### Key Architectural Considerations for Field Apps

#### 1. Background Sync API (Service Workers)
The standard `online` event listener only works if the app is **currently open**. To sync data while the browser is closed:
* Use a **Service Worker** with the [Background Sync API](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Background_Sync_API). This allows the browser to wake up the app and perform the API request even if the user has navigated away.

#### 2. Conflict Resolution
In field surveying, two people might edit the same record.
* **Timestamping:** Always store `created_at` and `updated_at` on the client side.
* **Server-side Logic:** If you receive a sync, check the server record version. If the server is newer, reject the sync or create a "Conflict" flag for the user to resolve manually.

#### 3. Optimistic UI
Don't wait for the server to confirm the save. Update your local state immediately (using React Query or SWR) so the field surveyor feels the app is snappy, even in remote locations.

#### 4. Data Integrity
* **Validation:** Validate data locally using `Zod` or `Yup` before saving to IndexedDB. You don't want to sync malformed data hours later.
* **Images:** If your survey includes photos, store the `Blob` in IndexedDB. Note: IndexedDB has size limits (usually 50% of free disk space), which is plenty for surveys, but avoid storing massive videos.

### Recommended Tech Stack
* **Frontend:** React or Vue.js
* **Offline Storage:** [Dexie.js](https://dexie.org/) (best wrapper for IndexedDB)
* **Sync Logic:** [TanStack Query](https://tanstack.com/query/latest) (handles caching and retries out of the box)
* **PWA:** [Vite PWA Plugin](https://vite-pwa-netlify.netlify.app/) (to ensure the app is installable and works offline)

