            <input type="number" id="value" placeholder="Value" required><br><br>
            <button type="submit">Update Chart</button>
        </form>
    </div>

    <!-- Chart Section -->
    <div id="chart-container">
        <canvas id="myChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <script>
        const ctx = document.getElementById('myChart').getContext('2d');

        // Initialize Chart
        const myChart = new Chart(ctx, {
            type: 'bar',
            data: {
                labels: ['Jan', 'Feb', 'Mar'],
                datasets: [{
                    label: 'Sales Data',
                    data: [12, 19, 3],
                    backgroundColor: 'rgba(54, 162, 235, 0.5)'
                }]
            }
        });

        // Form Handling
        document.getElementById('dataForm').addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
            e.preventDefault();

            const label = document.getElementById('label').value;
            const value = document.getElementById('value').value;

            // Update Chart Logic
            myChart.data.labels.push(label);
            myChart.data.datasets[0].data.push(value);

            // Re-render
            myChart.update();

            // Clear form
            e.target.reset();
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Key Components of this Solution:

1.  **Chart.js**: A high-performance library that handles the rendering logic. Using `myChart.update()` allows the UI to reflect changes without a page refresh.
2.  **DOM Manipulation**: We use `addEventListener` on the form. By calling `e.preventDefault()`, we stop the browser from reloading the page when the button is clicked, keeping the interaction "Single Page Application" style.
3.  **State Management**: The chart instance (`myChart`) acts as our "state." We push new values directly into the object's `data` arrays.

