```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Real-Time Dashboard</title>
    <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/chart.js"></script>
    <style>
        body { font-family: sans-serif; display: flex; padding: 20px; gap: 20px; }
        .panel { border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; width: 300px; }
        .chart-container { flex-grow: 1; height: 400px; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <div class="panel">
        <h3>Add Data Point</h3>
        <form id="dataForm">
            <input type="number" id="val" placeholder="Value" required><br><br>
            <button type="submit">Update Chart</button>
        </form>
        <p>Live Avg: <span id="avg">0</span></p>
    </div>

    <div class="chart-container">
        <canvas id="myChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <script>
        const ctx = document.getElementById('myChart').getContext('2d');
        const chart = new Chart(ctx, {
            type: 'line',
            data: {
                labels: [],
                datasets: [{ label: 'Live Data', data: [], borderColor: 'blue', fill: false }]
            },
            options: { responsive: true, maintainAspectRatio: false }
        });

        // Form Handling
        document.getElementById('dataForm').addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
            e.preventDefault();
            const val = parseFloat(document.getElementById('val').value);

            // Update Chart
            const now = new Date().toLocaleTimeString();
            chart.data.labels.push(now);
            chart.data.datasets[0].data.push(val);

            // Keep only last 10 points
            if(chart.data.labels.length > 10) {
                chart.data.labels.shift();
                chart.data.datasets[0].data.shift();
            }

            chart.update();
            updateStats();
        });

        function updateStats() {
            const data = chart.data.datasets[0].data;
            const avg = data.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / data.length;
            document.getElementById('avg').innerText = avg.toFixed(2);
        }

        // Simulate real-time stream
        setInterval(() => {
            const randomVal = Math.floor(Math.random() * 50);
            document.getElementById('val').value = randomVal;
            document.getElementById('dataForm').dispatchEvent(new Event('submit'));
        }, 3000);
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Key Components of this Solution:

1.  **Chart.js (Visualization):** A lightweight library that handles the rendering. We use a `line` chart configuration.
2.  **Form Handling:** We use `addEventListener('submit', ...)` to capture user input. Using `e.preventDefault()` stops the page from refreshing, allowing the dashboard to update smoothly.
3.  **Data Management:** 
    *   `chart.data.labels.push()` and `chart.data.datasets[0].data.push()` adds new data.
    *   `shift()` is used to keep the chart clean by removing old data points once the array exceeds 10 items.
4.  **Real-Time Simulation:** The `setInterval` function mimics an API connection by generating random data every 3 seconds and programmatically triggering the form submission.

### How to extend this for production:
*   **WebSockets:** Replace the `setInterval` logic with a WebSocket connection (using `new WebSocket('ws://your-api-url')`) to receive actual live data from a backend server.
*   **Data Persistence:** Integrate a local database like **IndexedDB** or send the data via `fetch()` requests to a REST API.
*   **Frameworks:** If you need a more complex dashboard, transition this logic into **React** or **Vue.js**, which handle state-to-view updates more efficiently as your app grows.

