### 3. The Logic (`app.js`)
This script initializes Firebase, renders tasks, and handles drag-and-drop events.

```javascript
import { initializeApp } from "https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/10.7.1/firebase-app.js";
import { getFirestore, collection, addDoc, onSnapshot, doc, updateDoc } from "https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/10.7.1/firebase-firestore.js";

const firebaseConfig = { /* YOUR FIREBASE CONFIG HERE */ };
const db = getFirestore(initializeApp(firebaseConfig));

// 1. Real-time sync from Firestore
onSnapshot(collection(db, "tasks"), (snapshot) => {
    document.querySelectorAll('.task-list').forEach(el => el.innerHTML = '');
    snapshot.forEach((doc) => {
        const task = doc.data();
        const el = document.createElement('div');
        el.className = "bg-white p-2 mb-2 rounded shadow cursor-move";
        el.innerText = task.text;
        el.dataset.id = doc.id;
        document.querySelector(`[data-status="${task.status}"]`).appendChild(el);
    });
});

// 2. Setup Drag and Drop
document.querySelectorAll('.task-list').forEach(list => {
    new Sortable(list, {
        group: 'shared',
        onEnd: async (evt) => {
            const taskId = evt.item.dataset.id;
            const newStatus = evt.to.dataset.status;
            await updateDoc(doc(db, "tasks", taskId), { status: newStatus });
        }
    });
});

// 3. Add Task
window.addTask = async () => {
    const text = prompt("Task name:");
    if (text) {
        await addDoc(collection(db, "tasks"), { text, status: "todo" });
    }
};
```

### Key Technical Concepts Used:

*   **Real-time Synchronization:** Because we use `onSnapshot` from Firestore, when any user drags a card, the `updateDoc` call fires. Every other client connected to the app will trigger their `onSnapshot` listener automatically and re-render the board. This creates the "real-time" experience without manually refreshing.
*   **Drag-and-Drop:** `SortableJS` handles the visual interaction. We attach the `onEnd` event to detect when a card has moved to a new column, then sync that state to the database.
*   **State Management:** The "Single Source of Truth" is the Firebase database. The UI is just a visual representation of that data.

### How to take this further:
1.  **Authentication:** Use `firebase/auth` to enable user logins so you can track *who* moved the task.
2.  **Optimistic UI:** Currently, it waits for Firebase to confirm the move. You can optimize by updating the local DOM immediately and reverting if the network call fails.
3.  **Advanced UI:** Replace `prompt()` with a custom modal, and add color-coding for priority levels.
4.  **Security Rules:** In your Firebase console, set up Firestore rules to ensure only logged-in users can read/write to the `tasks` collection.

