To create a minimal, functional version of a collaborative board, we will use:
1. **HTML5 Drag and Drop API** (for moving tasks).
2. **Firebase Realtime Database** (the easiest way to handle "real-time" without setting up a backend server).

### 1. Prerequisites
Create a project on [Firebase Console](https://console.firebase.google.com/), enable **Realtime Database**, and set rules to `read, write: true` for development.

### 2. The Code (`index.html`)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <style>
        .board { display: flex; gap: 20px; }
        .column { width: 200px; background: #eee; padding: 10px; min-height: 400px; }
        .task { background: white; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 5px; cursor: grab; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="board">
    <div class="column" id="todo" ondrop="drop(event)" ondragover="allowDrop(event)"><h3>To Do</h3></div>
    <div class="column" id="done" ondrop="drop(event)" ondragover="allowDrop(event)"><h3>Done</h3></div>
</div>

<script type="module">
    import { initializeApp } from "https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/9.6.1/firebase-app.js";
    import { getDatabase, ref, onValue, update } from "https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/9.6.1/firebase-database.js";

    const firebaseConfig = { /* PASTE YOUR CONFIG FROM FIREBASE */ };
    const db = getDatabase(initializeApp(firebaseConfig));

    // Render tasks from Firebase
    onValue(ref(db, 'tasks'), (snapshot) => {
        const tasks = snapshot.val() || {};
        document.querySelectorAll('.column').forEach(c => c.innerHTML = `<h3>${c.id}</h3>`);
        Object.entries(tasks).forEach(([id, status]) => {
            const el = document.createElement('div');
            el.className = 'task'; el.id = id; el.draggable = true;
            el.innerText = id;
            el.ondragstart = (e) => e.dataTransfer.setData("text", e.target.id);
            document.getElementById(status).appendChild(el);
        });
    });

    // Global Drop Handlers
    window.allowDrop = (e) => e.preventDefault();
    window.drop = (e) => {
        const id = e.dataTransfer.getData("text");
        const newStatus = e.target.closest('.column').id;
        update(ref(db, 'tasks'), { [id]: newStatus });
    };
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### 3. How it works:

1.  **State Synchronization:** Instead of updating the DOM directly, the `drop` function updates the **Firebase Realtime Database**. 
2.  **The Observer Pattern:** The `onValue` listener automatically triggers whenever the database changes. It clears the board and re-renders the DOM based on the new data. This is what makes it "Real-time"—if you open the page in two tabs, moving a task in one will instantly move it in the other.
3.  **Minimalist Persistence:** By using a simple key-value object (`{ "Task 1": "todo" }`), we keep the backend logic to a few lines of code.

### 4. How to run this:
1. Paste your specific Firebase config object (from the Firebase project dashboard) into the `firebaseConfig` variable.
2. Serve this file using a local web server (like the **Live Server** extension in VS Code) because Firebase requires a proper origin to authenticate.
3. Open the file in two different browser windows to see the real-time synchronization in action.

