### 1. The Core: Lock-Free Ring Buffer
We use a single-producer, single-consumer (SPSC) ring buffer. This avoids mutex contention entirely.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <atomic>
#include <vector>
#include <chrono>

struct Message {
    uint64_t id;
    uint64_t timestamp_ns;
    char data[64];
};

template<size_t Capacity>
class RingBuffer {
    Message buffer[Capacity];
    std::atomic<size_t> head{0};
    std::atomic<size_t> tail{0};

public:
    bool push(const Message& msg) {
        size_t t = tail.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        size_t next_t = (t + 1) % Capacity;
        if (next_t == head.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) return false;
        buffer[t] = msg;
        tail.store(next_t, std::memory_order_release);
        return true;
    }

    bool pop(Message& msg) {
        size_t h = head.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        if (h == tail.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) return false;
        msg = buffer[h];
        head.store((h + 1) % Capacity, std::memory_order_release);
        return true;
    }
};
```

### 2. High-Frequency Producer & Consumer
We pin threads to CPU cores (affinity) to prevent context switching, which is the "silent killer" of latency.

