### Key Optimizations for Gigabit Scale

#### 1. Pinning Threads (CPU Affinity)
Network processing is cache-sensitive. If your thread migrates cores, your L1/L2 cache becomes useless.
```c
cpu_set_t cpuset;
CPU_ZERO(&cpuset);
CPU_SET(target_core, &cpuset);
pthread_setaffinity_np(pthread_self(), sizeof(cpu_set_t), &cpuset);
```

#### 2. Hugepages
Standard memory pages are 4KB. Using **Hugepages (2MB or 1GB)** reduces TLB (Translation Lookaside Buffer) misses, which is critical when processing millions of packets per second.

#### 3. Vectorization (SIMD)
Do not process bytes one-by-one. Use Intel **AVX-512** or **NEON** instructions to parse multiple packet headers in parallel within a single CPU cycle.

#### 4. Batching
Never process one packet at a time. Always read/process in batches (e.g., 32 or 64 packets). This matches the CPU cache line architecture and minimizes the overhead of ring buffer updates.

#### 5. BPF Filtering (Kernel Side)
Offload as much work as possible to the kernel using **eBPF**. If you only care about specific traffic (e.g., TCP port 80), use an XDP program to drop or redirect traffic before it even reaches your user-space application.

