You must use **AF_PACKET with Memory Mapping (PACKET_RX_RING)** or **DPDK**. Below is a minimal, performance-oriented implementation using `mmap`'d raw sockets in C, which allows the kernel to write packet data directly into a buffer shared with your application.

### Minimal "Zero-Copy" Packet Sniffer (Linux)

This code sets up a circular buffer in memory. The kernel writes packets here; the application simply reads the memory addresses.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <linux/if_packet.h>
#include <net/ethernet.h>
#include <sys/mman.h>
#include <net/if.h>

#define RING_FRAMES 1024
#define FRAME_SIZE 2048

int main() {
    int fd = socket(AF_PACKET, SOCK_RAW, htons(ETH_P_ALL));

    // 1. Setup RX_RING
    struct tpacket_req req = {
        .tp_block_size = FRAME_SIZE * RING_FRAMES,
        .tp_frame_size = FRAME_SIZE,
        .tp_block_nr = 1,
        .tp_frames_per_block = RING_FRAMES
    };
    setsockopt(fd, SOL_PACKET, PACKET_RX_RING, &req, sizeof(req));

    // 2. Map kernel buffer to user space
    uint8_t *ring = mmap(NULL, req.tp_block_size, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE, 
                         MAP_SHARED, fd, 0);

    struct tpacket_hdr *header = (struct tpacket_hdr *)ring;
    int i = 0;

    printf("Monitoring traffic...\n");

    // 3. Busy-wait loop (Performance critical: no syscalls here)
    while (1) {
        // Check if kernel has released the packet to us
        if (header->tp_status & TP_STATUS_USER) {
            // Process packet at (uint8_t*)header + header->tp_mac
            printf("Packet captured: %d bytes\n", header->tp_snaplen);

            // Release back to kernel
            header->tp_status = TP_STATUS_KERNEL;

            // Advance to next frame
            i = (i + 1) % RING_FRAMES;
            header = (struct tpacket_hdr *)(ring + (i * FRAME_SIZE));
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
```

### Critical Performance Strategies

To scale this to full line-rate gigabit (or 10Gbps+), you must implement the following:

#### 1. Avoid Syscalls in the Hot Path
The example above uses `TP_STATUS_USER` polling. Do **not** use `poll()` or `select()`. Use a busy-wait loop to check the status flags in the mapped memory. This keeps the CPU core at 100% but eliminates the latency of context switching.

#### 2. CPU Pinning (Affinity)
A network flow analyzer should be bound to a specific CPU core to prevent the OS scheduler from moving the process and flushing the L1/L2 cache.
```c
cpu_set_t cpuset;
CPU_ZERO(&cpuset);
CPU_SET(1, &cpuset); // Pin to core 1
sched_setaffinity(0, sizeof(cpu_set_t), &cpuset);
```

#### 3. RSS (Receive Side Scaling)
For gigabit traffic, a single thread will struggle to parse every packet. You should configure the NIC to spread flows across multiple hardware queues (RSS) and run one instance of this code per hardware queue, pinned to a unique CPU core.

#### 4. Bypass Kernel (The "Pro" Path)
If you need to hit >10 Gbps, stop using Linux sockets entirely. Use **DPDK (Data Plane Development Kit)** or **AF_XDP**. 
*   **AF_XDP** is the modern Linux standard: It uses an eBPF program to redirect packets directly from the NIC driver into your user-space application, completely bypassing the Linux network stack.

