To create a minimal physics plugin, we must define the two pillars of any physics engine: **The Integration** (updating positions) and **The Collision** (resolving overlaps).

Since most commercial engines (Unity, Unreal) use C++, I will provide a **minimal C++ framework** that you can drop into an engine’s "Component" system.

### 1. The Physics Component (`PhysicsObject.h`)
Every object needs velocity and mass.

```cpp
struct PhysicsObject {
    float mass = 1.0f;
    Vector3 position;
    Vector3 velocity;
    Vector3 acceleration;

    void Integrate(float deltaTime) {
        // Simple Euler Integration
        velocity += acceleration * deltaTime;
        position += velocity * deltaTime;
        acceleration = {0, 0, 0}; // Reset force accumulator
    }
};
```

### 2. The Collision Primitive (`AABB.h`)
To detect collisions, we use Axis-Aligned Bounding Boxes (AABB).

```cpp
struct AABB {
    Vector3 min; // Bottom-left-back
    Vector3 max; // Top-right-front

    bool Intersects(const AABB& other) const {
        return (min.x <= other.max.x && max.x >= other.min.x) &&
               (min.y <= other.max.y && max.y >= other.min.y) &&
               (min.z <= other.max.z && max.z >= other.min.z);
    }
};
```

### 3. The Physics World (The "Plugin" Core)
This acts as the manager that loops through all objects, calculates forces (gravity), and resolves collisions.

```cpp
class PhysicsWorld {
    std::vector<PhysicsObject*> objects;

    void Step(float dt) {
        for (auto* obj : objects) {
            // Apply gravity
            obj->acceleration.y += -9.81f; 
            obj->Integrate(dt);
        }

        // Simple O(n^2) Collision detection
        for (size_t i = 0; i < objects.size(); ++i) {
            for (size_t j = i + 1; j < objects.size(); ++j) {
                if (CheckCollision(objects[i], objects[j])) {
                    ResolveCollision(objects[i], objects[j]);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    void ResolveCollision(PhysicsObject* a, PhysicsObject* b) {
        // Minimal response: Stop motion (Perfect inelastic collision)
        a->velocity = {0, 0, 0};
        b->velocity = {0, 0, 0};
    }
};
```

